DEPENDENCIES

  (executables)
    perl    (1) - The Perl 5 Language Interpreter
    git     (1) - the stupid content tracker
  (Perl 5 modules)
    Capture::Tiny - Capture STDOUT and STDERR
    CGI         - Common Gateway Interface requests and responses
    JSON::XS    - JSON serialising/deserialising

You should have installed these using your system package manager.
For the Perl modules, you can install using cpan(1p), or cpanm(1p)
optionally in a local environment set up with local::lib.  If you
use cpanm(1p), there is a convenient command you can run:

    cpanm --installdeps <this directory>

or if your current working directory is this directory,
you may simply run `cpanm --installdeps .'.

Occasionally, you may use the Perl modules provided by your system
package manager; for instance, on Debian-based distros, you can do

    apt-get install libcgi-pm-perl libjson-xs-perl

INSTALLATION

Run:

    perl Makefile.PL
    perl Makefile.PL INSTALL_BASE=/srv/www/cgi/lib/<place>

Specify INSTALL_BASE if you don't want it to go into the
default base directory.  Then repeat the mantra:

    make
    make test
    make install

(Hint: You might get more colorful tests if you run `prove -b' intead)

DEVELOPMENT

You do almost the same thing, except...

    cpanm --with-feature=hacker --installdeps <this directory>

Then instead of `make test', run something more comprehensive:

    prove -b t/* xt/*
